course: dictionary
lesson: 7324
title: Plait: a word with two jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A plait can be a thing or an action. As a noun, a plait is a length of hair, rope, bread or cloth made from strands crossed over each other. As a verb, to plait means to make that shape by crossing the strands. «Chloe's plaits are pulled so hard she cries» uses plaits as things. «Gabi plaits her hair» uses plaits as an action.
explain: Look at the words around plait to find out which job it does. A little word like «a» or «her» often goes just before a noun, so «a plait» or «her plaits» tells you it is a thing. Words like «plaits her hair» show someone doing something, so plaits is a verb here. Try putting in «makes» or «crosses» to check.
explain: The word is often linked with feelings. «Maddie grabbed both of Chloe's long plaits and yanked» shows that pulling someone's plaits can be unkind. In stories this is a classic picture of teasing or bullying, so the context tells you more than the dictionary alone.
fact: A plait is a length of hair, rope, bread or cloth with three or more strands crossed over each other again and again.
fact: As a verb, to plait means to cross three or more strands over each other to make this shape.
fact: Plaits are a common school hairstyle, especially for long hair.
fact: «In plaits», «a French plait» and «a plaited loaf» are phrases you may meet.
fact: In stories, «he pulled her plaits» is a picture of teasing or bullying.
exercise: «Chloe's plaits are pulled so hard she cries.» In this sentence, what is a plait? | *A length of hair crossed over in three strands | A type of cake | A loud shout | A ribbon tied in a bow
exercise: «Her nanny, Gabi, plaits her hair every day.» What does plaits mean here? | To cut short | To pull hard | *To cross three strands over each other | To wash and dry
exercise: «And Sam tells Maddie she should not have pulled Chloe's plaits.» Why is this a sign of teasing? | It is a story about bread | *It shows someone being unkind to another child | It means Sam is going to the shop | It shows someone pulling a plait, which is unkind
test: «Next night the Woman dries sweet grass, plaits a halter and makes the Second Singing Magic.» Is plaits a noun or a verb here? | Adjective, because it describes the grass | Adverb, because it tells how | Noun, because it is a thing | *Verb, because the Woman is doing something
test: Which sentence uses plait as a noun? | She plaits every morning | *Chloe's long plaits were yanked | The baker will plait the dough | Gabi will plait her hair tomorrow
exam: Explain in one sentence how you know whether plait is a noun or a verb in «Maddie grabbed both of Chloe's long plaits and yanked.» | It is a verb because it ends with s | It is a verb because grabbed is a verb | *The little word "of" and the words "Chloe's long" come before plaits, so it is a thing | It is a noun because yanked is a noun
